WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:01.490
- Hi guys.

00:00:02.739 --> 00:00:04.020
- How's it going?
- Hey.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:05.720
- Oh my gosh, okay.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:07.980
Liz, hi, how are you?

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:09.660
- I'm good, how are you?

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:10.710
- I'm really great.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:13.370
I love your album so much and I can't wait

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:16.110
for Christmas to come
because I wanna decorate

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:20.670
the Christmas tree with my
daughters and put on the album.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:24.390
And it feels obviously perennial,

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:27.230
but it just has this classic,

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:32.230
wonderful, holiday, old world era music.

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:35.990
And Seth, you've always been that guy.

00:00:35.990 --> 00:00:39.060
In fact, Seth and I, fun fact everyone,

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.410
used to have lots of cocktails

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:44.640
and go karaokeing with groups of friends.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.830
And I miss those days so much, Seth.

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:50.800
- Yeah, it's, I miss it too.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.010
You know, that was fun.

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:55.220
We used to go to this spot
called the Brass Monkey

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:57.370
in Los Angeles every Monday night,

00:00:57.370 --> 00:00:58.910
after work at "Family Guy".

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:00.410
And that's actually where I met Liz.

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:02.750
- Well, you know, I wouldn't be surprised

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:05.380
if you two were from another era,

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:09.377
because when I listen
to your beautiful album,

00:01:09.377 --> 00:01:10.560
"Songs From Home".

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.510
How did this come about?

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:15.970
And where were you guys
when you recorded it?

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:18.464
- Liz, the whole thing
was actually Liz's idea.

00:01:18.464 --> 00:01:19.297
- Was it?

00:01:19.297 --> 00:01:20.350
- This, she's, you know, we're all,

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:22.010
we're stuck in this quarantine.

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:27.010
We're not doing anything with
our little silly abilities.

00:01:27.424 --> 00:01:29.950
And so, why don't we make some music?

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:34.090
And, we have, it's a
very interesting setup

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:39.090
where each musician,
oftentimes 50 or 60 of them,

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:42.010
are all recording in their homes.

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:43.490
We have a very talented engineer,

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:47.740
Rich Breen, who mixes all
of these tracks together.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:51.820
And it makes it sound like the
orchestra that it in fact is.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:54.403
And then she records in
Atlanta and I record in LA.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:56.793
- Well, Seth, I think you know

00:01:56.793 --> 00:01:59.450
the most talented musicians in the world.

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:02.200
And I think that they play a huge part.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:06.450
And then, I guess, having
setups on either coast, sort of,

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:08.300
not that I'm technically
on a coast, really helped.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:11.530
And Seth would record first and
then I would record my part.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:13.010
And then we kinda just took it from there.

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:15.740
And it worked out, thank God,
because it was a lot of fun.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:18.160
It made quarantine a
lot less dull, for sure.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:20.080
- So Liz, you would
take your peanut butter,

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:21.860
and Seth would take his chocolate,

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:23.230
and then you would put it together

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:25.680
and make musical Reese's Pieces.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.810
- Beautiful jazz sandwich, yes.

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:31.220
- Seth, you've always been
a fan of this era of music.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:34.650
Did you think that this is
just another perfect time

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.453
to put out this old world classic music?

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:39.493
- Yeah.

00:02:39.493 --> 00:02:41.660
I mean, again, Liz and I used to

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.080
sing with symphony orchestras.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.060
I would go, I would travel
around with Joel McNeely,

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:53.750
who's my conductor, and Liz
would come and sing some duets.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:56.429
With the Chicago symphony,
with the Philadelphia symphony,

00:02:56.429 --> 00:02:57.750
the San Francisco symphony.

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:00.500
So we've been doing this
actually for a while,

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:01.780
for a long time actually.

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:04.210
And we hadn't ever
actually recorded anything

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:07.896
except one song about Buicks.

00:03:07.896 --> 00:03:09.160
(everyone laughs)

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:10.583
- I love a good Buick.

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:12.483
- Don't we all?

00:03:12.483 --> 00:03:15.560
And you know, my fans, they're a bit of a

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:17.581
different demographic
than Seth's fan base.

00:03:17.581 --> 00:03:18.830
And that's what was really cool too,

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:20.280
to bring those two together.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.633
And this was new for
them, this style of music,

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:25.020
they hadn't listened to it that much.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:28.910
So to introduce them, and
see how much they love it,

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:31.860
and now how much they look
forward to it was really awesome.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:34.160
- It's also rad to hear like crooner music

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:36.960
being said it's gonna drop every week.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.993
You sort of mix the peanut
butter and the chocolate

00:03:38.993 --> 00:03:42.180
of the old world and the new world.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:46.550
Before we go, Seth, I just have to say,

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:49.510
hi from Jillian, how's Brian?

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:51.280
- Oh, yes, that's right, that's right.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:52.400
From "Family Guy".

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:53.490
Yes, that's right.

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:56.940
- I mean, I had so many, so many fun years

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:59.730
of coming into the studio
on Wilshire Boulevard

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:02.080
and recording in the booth with you.

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:04.130
I was always doing fun things.

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:06.100
And then you told me, all right,

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.770
now you're gonna play Jillian,
who's Brian's girlfriend.

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:10.560
And she's sort of an airhead.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.630
And she talks like this.

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:14.990
And I have to say, it's sadly

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:17.270
not that far off from my own voice.

00:04:17.270 --> 00:04:21.600
And I miss Brian and
Jillian, Seth, I really do.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:26.600
By the way, Seth, how's
the cousin thing going?

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:27.900
- Yes, that's right.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:28.733
Yes, yeah.

00:04:28.733 --> 00:04:32.700
Drew and I are distant cousins,
we discovered years ago.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.426
- How crazy is that?
- Wow.

00:04:35.426 --> 00:04:38.550
- It's somewhere, the
Barrymores and the MacFarlanes,

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:40.300
there's, this was like years ago,

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:41.710
my dad did our family tree.

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:44.240
And he was like, oh, oh
my god, look at this.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.970
It's distant, it's like
a fourth or fifth cousin,

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:48.160
I think that's the relation.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:52.670
But it's there.

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:54.180
- You know what's so funny?

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:56.410
I think he discovered them drinking

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:59.420
and doing karaoke all those years ago.

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:02.130
And that's why we had
to bring it full circle

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:03.763
and keep it alive.
- That's right.

00:05:03.763 --> 00:05:05.480
Karaoke's been around since the Mayflower.

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:08.190
- Well, I know that I've always had fun

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:09.870
hanging out with you, Seth.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:13.500
And Liz, everyone at our
company loves you so much.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:16.340
We had the privilege of
working with you years ago.

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:18.520
And we all celebrate you guys.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:21.320
And we're so excited to
talk about this album.

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:25.340
It's a beautiful soundtrack
for our lives right now.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:28.210
So thank you for putting
another wonderful thing

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:29.870
out there into the world, you guys.

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:31.347
Thank you.
- Thank you.

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:33.170
- Thanks, Drew.

00:05:33.170 --> 00:05:36.370
- Thank you guys so much
for coming on the show.

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:38.390
Liz Gillies and Seth MacFarlane.

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:40.197
Their new collection of songs,

00:05:40.197 --> 00:05:43.830
"Songs From Home", now
available on Spotify.

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:44.750
They're all out.

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:46.977
And Seth has a new album as well,

00:05:46.977 --> 00:05:49.570
"Great Songs From the Stage and Screen".

00:05:49.570 --> 00:05:51.410
And that's available right now too.

00:05:51.410 --> 00:05:53.610
Guess what I will be listening to

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:55.420
throughout the rest of the year as well.

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:56.513
Thank you, Seth.

00:05:56.513 --> 00:05:59.096
(upbeat music)

